## 4) `OUTPUT_SCHEMA.md`

```markdown
# Benchmark Output Schema (Required Artefacts)

This document defines what a benchmark run must persist so results can be audited and compared later.

It intentionally does not mandate a specific directory root; it defines what files and fields must exist.

---

## 1) Required persisted artefacts (per run)

### 1.1 Config snapshot
A single file capturing:
- benchmark name + version identifier
- solver configuration (including Stage-1 vs Stage-2 mode flags)
- scoring configuration (metric families used; n values; direction; any transforms; WLI definition variant)
- normalisation policy and weights (if combining scores)
- candidate retention settings (K, top_k source, etc.)
- random baseline definition (random sample size, RNG seed(s), key-family definition)
- runtime matrix / instance selection (IDs or hashes as appropriate)

### 1.2 Per-instance results table (machine-readable)
A CSV or JSONL table with at least:
- instance identifier (stable id or hash)
- text identifier (if multiple texts)
- key seed identifier (if multiple key seeds)
- mode identifier (Stage-1 only, Stage-2A0, Stage-2A wli2_full_stream, Stage-2A wli2_within_word, etc.)
- budgets:
  - score evaluation counts (where available)
  - wall time
- recovery metrics:
  - match_ratio
  - (and any stronger recovery metric if available)
- per-metric family scores:
  - oracle_*, median_random_*, best_random_*, best_sol_*
  - oracle_gap_* for each metric family
  - oracle_percentile_among_random, sep_z

### 1.3 Candidate set (deterministic, size K)
Persist the retained candidates used for best-K evaluation:
- Prefer keys.
- If keys are not available, store plaintext indices plus a stable key identifier if possible.

Minimum contents per candidate:
- rank
- key representation (or key hash) and/or plaintext indices
- scores used for ranking (per metric family)
- recovery metric(s) when available

### 1.4 Summary report (human-readable)
A Markdown (or plain text) summary containing:
- brief run summary (matrix size, modes, budgets)
- aggregated tables (p10/p50/p90 for match_ratio; oracle_gap distributions)
- trust gate status per mode/metric
- notes: any hard abort reason must be captured clearly

---

## 2) Recommended directory layout (example)

- run_root/
  - config.json
  - results.csv
  - candidates/
    - instance_<id>__mode_<mode>.json
  - summary.md
  - logs/
    - benchmark.log

This layout is only an example. The requirement is that equivalent artefacts exist and are findable.

---

## 3) Reproducibility requirements

- All RNG seeds used for:
  - random baselines
  - solver initialisation (if applicable)
  - candidate retention selection (if applicable)
  must be recorded in the config snapshot.

- If a run depends on external assets (LM tables, ECDF tables), the run must record:
  - asset identifiers and versions (hashes/paths) sufficient to detect drift.
```

